## Overview  

`app.tsx` is the **core layout module** of the website.  
Its job is to:

1. **Load every page** that lives in the `../pages` folder (all of them are written in MDX).  
2. **Describe each page** (its headings, order, name, and the section it belongs to).  
3. **Group the pages into sections** so the side‑navigation can be built automatically.  
4. **Expose a helper** (`getEditUrl`) that creates a link to edit the current page on GitHub.  
5. **Export a React component** (`App`) that renders the whole page‑shell (header, footer, side‑nav, main content, etc.).

Below is a line‑by‑line walk‑through of what the file does.

---

## 1️⃣ Imports  

```tsx
import {
  RootLayout,
  VerticalStack,
  Heading,
  mdxComponents,
  ToAnchor,
  AnchorProvider,
  colors,
  PageLink,
  Text,
} from '@compiled/website-ui';
import { MDXProvider } from '@mdx-js/react';
import { useLocation } from 'react-router-dom';

import { titleCase } from '../utils/string';

import { PageTitle } from './page-title';
import { ScrollTop } from './scroll-top';
import { LinkItem, Section } from './side-nav';
```

| Import | What it is | Why it is used in this file |
|--------|------------|-----------------------------|
| **RootLayout** | The top‑level layout component supplied by the UI library. | Wraps the whole page (header + footer + content area). |
| **VerticalStack** | A simple flex container that arranges children vertically. | Lays out the side‑navigation next to the main content. |
| **Heading** | A styled heading component (h1‑h6). | Used inside pages to render section headings. |
| **mdxComponents** | An object that tells the MDX parser how to render raw Markdown elements (e.g., `<h1>`, `<pre>`). | Allows the page’s MDX content to be turned into React elements. |
| **ToAnchor** | Helper that creates an `<a>` that scrolls to an element with a matching `id`. | Used by the side‑nav to link to headings inside a page. |
| **AnchorProvider** | Context provider that makes `ToAnchor` work. | Needed for the `ToAnchor` helper. |
| **colors** | Design‑system colour tokens. | Used for CSS‑in‑JS styling (e.g., the `css` prop). |
| **PageLink** | A link component that automatically adds the right classes. | Could be used for navigation inside pages. |
| **Text** | Plain text component with built‑in styling. | For rendering simple text nodes. |
| **MDXProvider** | React context that makes MDX parsing available to the component tree. | Wraps the page content so the MDX can be rendered. |
| **useLocation** | React‑Router hook that gives the current URL (`location`). | Needed for `getEditUrl` to read the current route. |
| **titleCase** | Small utility that converts a string to “Title Case”. | Used when generating readable section names. |
| **PageTitle** | Custom component that displays the page’s title (taken from the page’s front‑matter). | Shown at the top of each page. |
| **ScrollTop** | Button that scrolls the page back to the top. | Placed in the layout for user convenience. |
| **LinkItem** | A navigation item used by the side‑nav. | Represents each link in the side‑nav menu. |
| **Section** | Type that describes a section (its name and the pages it contains). | Used by `getSections` to type‑check the returned structure. |

---

## 2️⃣ `requireAllPages` – loading every page dynamically  

```tsx
function requireAllPages() {
  const req = require.context('../pages');   // Webpack’s way to load files from a folder
  return req.keys().reduce(
    (acc, filename) =>
      Object.assign(acc, {
        // Strip the leading "./" and the ".mdx" extension
        [filename.replace('./', '').replace('.mdx', '')]: req(filename),
      }),
    {}
  );
}
```

* **What it does**  
  * `require.context('../pages')` creates a *context* that can `require` any file inside `../pages`.  
  * `req.keys()` returns an array of all matched file names (e.g., `"./about.mdx"`).  
  * The `reduce` builds a plain object where each key is the file name **without** the `./` prefix and **without** the `.mdx` suffix, and each value is the actual component that imports that file.

* **Why it matters**  
  The website is a *static‑site generator*‑style app: pages are separate MDX files. By using a context we can import **all** of them at runtime, then treat each as a React component. This makes the side‑nav and section logic completely data‑driven – we never have to hard‑code a list of page names.

---

## 3️⃣ `Page` interface  

```tsx
interface Page {
  default: React.ComponentType<Record<string, never>>;
  data: {
    headings: {
      depth: number;
      text: string;
    }[];
    order?: number;
    name?: string;
    section: string;
  };
}
```

* **`default`** – The component that renders the page’s MDX content.  
* **`data`** – Metadata that each page exports (the UI library expects this shape).  
  * **`headings`** – An array of the headings that appear in the page, each with its **depth** (1 = `<h1>`, 2 = `<h2>`, …) and the **text** that appears in the heading. This is used to build the side‑nav (the list of links that jump to each heading).  
  * **`order`** – Optional numeric order; if omitted the default is `100`. Pages are sorted by this value when building sections.  
  * **`name`** – Optional human‑readable name (e.g., “About”). If not supplied the file name (without extension) is used.  
  * **`section`** – The name of the *section* the page belongs to (e.g., “Intro”, “Features”). This is the key that `getSections` uses to group pages.

---

## 4️⃣ `getSections` – organise pages into sections  

```tsx
const getSections = () => {
  const pages: Record<string, Page> = requireAllPages(); // ← all imported pages

  const sections: Record<string, (Page & { name: string })[]> = {};

  // 1️⃣ Sort pages by their `order` (fallback 100) so that within a section they appear in the right sequence.
  Object.entries(pages)
    .sort((page1, page2) => (page1[1].data.order || 100) - (page2[1].data.order || 100))
    .forEach(([pageName, page]) => {
      const section = page.data.section;

      // 2️⃣ Validate that every page declares a section – otherwise we throw a clear error.
      if (!section) {
        throw new Error(
          `Put ${pageName}.mdx in a section! E.g:\n--- \nsection: My section\n---`
        );
      }

      // 3️⃣ Ensure the section array exists, then push the page (adding its `name` property).
      sections[section] = sections[section] || [];
      sections[section].push({
        ...page,
        name: pageName, // the file name (without .mdx) becomes the link text
      });
    });

  // 4️⃣ Sort the *sections* themselves.
  //    The section name is expected to start with a numeric prefix, e.g. "1-Intro".
  //    We extract that prefix, convert it to a number, and sort ascending.
  return Object.entries(sections)
    .sort(([a], [b]) => {
      const aOrder = Number(a.match(/^(\d+)-/)[1] || 100);
      const bOrder = Number(b.match(/^(\d+)-/)[1] || 100);
      return aOrder - bOrder;
    })
    .map(([name, pages]) => ({
      name,
      pages,
    }));
};
```

### What the function accomplishes

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Load pages** | Calls `requireAllPages()` to get a map of all page components. |
| **Sort by order** | Guarantees that, within a single section, pages appear in the order defined by their `order` property. |
| **Validate section** | Throws a helpful error if a page forgets to set `section`. This prevents silent mis‑grouping. |
| **Group by section** | Creates a nested object where each key is a section name and the value is an array of the pages that belong to it. Each page object is also enriched with a `name` property (the file name) so the side‑nav can display a readable label. |
| **Sort sections** | The section names are expected to start with a numeric prefix (`1-`, `2-`, …). The regex extracts that number, converts it to a float, and sorts the sections accordingly. This yields a deterministic order for the navigation menu. |
| **Return** | An array of `{ name, pages }` objects, ready to be fed into the side‑nav component. |

---

## 5️⃣ `getEditUrl` – create a GitHub edit link  

```tsx
const getEditUrl = () => {
  const name = location.pathname.split('/')[2] || 'installation';
  return `https://github.com/compiled/compiled-website/tree/
END OF app.tsx
```

* **How it works**  
  * `location.pathname` is the current URL path (e.g., `/section/2-about`).  
  * Splitting on “/” and taking the third element (`[2]`) gives the *file name* (e.g., `2-about`).  
  * If the path does not contain a third segment (maybe the home page), it falls back to the string `"installation"`.

* **Result**  
  The function returns a URL that points to the corresponding file in the GitHub repository, with the trailing `/tree/` part (the snippet you see is truncated, but the intention is to build a link like `https://github.com/.../tree/2-about`). Clicking that link opens the file in the GitHub UI for editing.

---

## 6️⃣ The `App` component (the exported UI)  

Although the snippet you posted ends before the actual `App` component definition, the typical pattern for this file is:

```tsx
const App = () => (
  <RootLayout>
    {/* The side‑nav is built from the sections we just computed */}
    <VerticalStack>
      {/* The side‑nav component receives the sections array */}
      <SideNav sections={getSections()} />

      {/* Main content area */}
      <MainContent>
        {/* The currently selected page component is rendered here */}
        {/* MDXProvider wraps the page so its markdown can be parsed */}
        <MDXProvider>{/* page component */}</MDXProvider>

        {/* Page title (taken from page.data.name or headings[0]) */}
        <PageTitle />

        {/* Scroll‑to‑top button */}
        <ScrollTop />
      </MainContent>
    </VerticalStack>
  </RootLayout>
);
export default App;
```

### What the component does (high‑level)

| Piece | Role |
|-------|------|
| **`RootLayout`** | Provides the overall page skeleton (header, footer, responsive breakpoints). |
| **`VerticalStack`** | Places the side‑nav next to the main content, handling the responsive layout (collapsed on small screens). |
| **`SideNav`** (not shown but implied) | Consumes the `sections` array returned by `getSections()`. It maps each section → its pages → renders a `LinkItem` for each heading, using `ToAnchor` to enable in‑page linking. |
| **`MainContent`** | Holds the page that matches the current route (`useLocation`). The appropriate page component (imported via `requireAllPages`) is rendered inside an `MDXProvider` so its markdown is turned into React elements. |
| **`PageTitle`** | Shows the page’s title (usually the first heading or the `data.name`). |
| **`ScrollTop`** | A small UI widget that lets the user jump back to the top of the page. |
| **`MDXProvider`** | Supplies the context needed for the MDX parser; without it the page’s markdown would not render. |

---

## 7️⃣ How everything fits together (the data flow)

1. **When the app boots** the `getSections` function runs once (or whenever it is called).  
2. It **loads every page** via `requireAllPages`, reads each page’s `data` (headings, order, section).  
3. It **groups pages** into sections, sorts both pages *inside* a section and the sections themselves.  
4. The **side‑nav component** receives this structured data and builds a clickable menu that reflects the site’s logical sections.  
5. The **`App` component** renders the layout, injects the side‑nav, and then renders whatever page the router says is active.  
6. When the user clicks a link in the side‑nav, the router changes the URL; the same `App` component re‑runs, re‑evaluates `getSections` (or a cached version), and the new page’s headings become the new navigation highlights.  
7. The **`getEditUrl`** helper can be called anywhere (e.g., a “Edit on GitHub” button) to give the user a direct link to edit the currently displayed page.

---

## 8️⃣ TL;DR (concise summary)

* **Imports** bring in UI components, MDX support, routing helpers, and small utilities.  
* **`requireAllPages`** dynamically imports every `.mdx` file from `../pages` and returns a map of file‑name → component.  
* **`Page` interface** describes the shape of each page’s exported data (headings, order, name, section).  
* **`getSections`** sorts pages by order, validates that each page belongs to a section, groups them, and returns a sorted list of sections with their pages.  
* **`getEditUrl`** builds a GitHub edit link based on the current route.  
* The **`App` component** (not fully shown but inferred) wires all of this together: it creates a layout with a side‑nav built from the sections, renders the active page’s MDX content, shows a title, and provides a scroll‑to‑top button.

In short, `app.tsx` is the **glue** that turns a collection of independent MDX pages into a cohesive, navigable website with automatic side‑navigation and a consistent layout.

